  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237869/assets/cce0e775722bbaaa8eeeb60a7cd097e8ad59b7c83c838f072416112aed657e33.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21}{4}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=3\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{3b}{4} \\\\ 3b & =21 \\\\ b & =7\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=7\\text{cm}.$",
